8834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1031 "The Cartesian Coordinate System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1033 92 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050517 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "50123c1c-7113-541e-8c4f-047f2eec416d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096431 #edb/ref 1069 "e-405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096431 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f0/f02616ea8f44865a727bec3affe596503dbadacafb113acad6e5f755218b4ff5.png)\n\nFor the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096431 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096432 #edb/ref 1069 "e-406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096432 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096432 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096432 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = 4-x,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(4-(1+\\frac{2k}{n}))\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(3-\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096433 #edb/ref 1069 "e-407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096433 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096433 #edb/ref 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}$ and $f(x_k) = x_{k}^2.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)= x^2$ and $b = 8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096434 #edb/ref 1069 "e-408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096434 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x$ as the limit of a right Riemann sum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096434 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = x^2,$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096630 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/r